One generates next a random number to decide whether the \(f^{0}\) decays to \(K^{+}K^{-}\) or \(K_{s}^{0}K_{s}^{0}\) or \(K_{l}^{0}K_{l}^{0}\). If this number is less than \(\frac{1}{4}\), the particle is decayed into \(K_{s}^{0}K_{s}^{0}\). The number of primary \(K_{s}^{0}\) is kept fixed for simplicity and the \(f^{0}\)'s are generated until \(f^{0}/K_{s}^{0}=c\) where \(0<c<1\) is some specified number. Using \(f(M_{i})\), the Briet-Wigner mass distribution given by Eq. (9.6), the mass of the \(f^{0}\) is picked by means of a normalized mass distribution function

\[F(M)=\frac{1}{F(M_{n})}\sum_{i=1}^{n}f(M_{i}) \tag{9.10}\]

